WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:09.887
There is a building in India that has been called many things: the most perfect building in the world, the throne of Allah, and the jewel of India.

2
00:00:09.888 --> 00:00:16.688
This building, in the city of Agra in northern India, is the Taj Mahal, which means "Crown Palace."

3
00:00:16.689 --> 00:00:24.283
It was built to be a grand tomb for the remains of Empress Mumtaz Mahal, born Arjumand Banu Begum.

4
00:00:24.284 --> 00:00:32.861
In 1632, the empress' husband, Shah Jahan, ordered the tomb to be built as an expression of love for his dead wife.

5
00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:45.850
With its magnificent architecture and extraordinary beauty, the Taj Mahal has amazed visitors and pilgrims for four centuries and is considered to be a modern wonder of the world.

6
00:00:45.851 --> 00:00:56.672
Today, India has many religions, including Islam, Hinduism, and Buddhism, but the Taj Mahal was built in a time when India was primarily an Islamic country.

7
00:00:56.673 --> 00:01:03.148
From 1526 to 1858, India was ruled by the Mughal emperors.

8
00:01:03.149 --> 00:01:09.086
The Mughals were Muslims and originally came from Persia, now the country of Iran.

9
00:01:09.087 --> 00:01:12.991
They brought Persian culture, architecture, and art to India.

10
00:01:12.992 --> 00:01:18.899
The man responsible for the Taj Mahal, Shah Jahan, was born as Prince Khurram.

11
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.819
He was well educated, very intelligent, and cultured.

12
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.478
When he was fifteen, Prince Khurram met a young woman at the palace.

13
00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:30.224
For both, it was love at first sight.

14
00:01:30.225 --> 00:01:36.291
This Persian woman was Arjumand Banu Begum, and the couple married five years later.

15
00:01:36.292 --> 00:01:43.791
They were inseparable, and Arjumand even accompanied her husband on military campaigns across the Indian empire.

16
00:01:43.792 --> 00:01:48.777
Their extraordinary love was documented by court poets and historians.

17
00:01:48.778 --> 00:01:57.111
After Prince Khurram's father died, he took over the empire and began to use the name "Shah Jahan," which means "King of the World."

18
00:01:57.112 --> 00:02:04.118
Even with her husband in this new position of power, Arjumand remained his friend, counsel, and lover.

19
00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:06.752
Together they had fourteen children.

20
00:02:06.753 --> 00:02:16.178
In 1631, when Shah Jahan and Arjumand had been married for eighteen years and he had been the emperor of India for three, tragedy struck.

21
00:02:16.179 --> 00:02:19.281
Arjumand was in her ninth month of pregnancy.

22
00:02:19.282 --> 00:02:26.434
Shah Jahan left the city of Agra to travel with his wife and army to Burhanpur, to put down a rebellion there.

23
00:02:26.435 --> 00:02:31.663
While they were away, Arjumand died while giving birth to their fourteenth child.

24
00:02:31.664 --> 00:02:34.004
Shah Jahan was devastated.

25
00:02:34.005 --> 00:02:39.834
The court historians wrote: "The king cried out with grief, like an ocean raging with storm . . .

26
00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:43.652
For the whole week afterward, His Majesty did not appear in public . . .

27
00:02:43.653 --> 00:02:48.323
From constant weeping, he was forced to use glasses, and his hair turned grey."

28
00:02:48.324 --> 00:02:56.573
The emperor grieved for two years, and historians recorded that he never showed enthusiasm for administering his empire again.

29
00:02:56.574 --> 00:03:01.073
Instead, Shah Jahan found solace in art and architecture.

30
00:03:01.074 --> 00:03:08.235
Six months after his wife's death, he began planning a magnificent memorial to his wife, a memorial to love.

31
00:03:08.236 --> 00:03:14.632
This memorial would consist of Arjumand's tomb, ornamental gardens, and several other buildings.

32
00:03:14.633 --> 00:03:22.300
Set on the banks of the Jamuna River near Shah Jahan's palace in Agra, the complex became the Taj Mahal.

33
00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:28.792
At this time, the Mughal Empire was at its peak and India was an extremely wealthy country.

34
00:03:28.793 --> 00:03:34.200
Consequently, Shah Jahan spared no expense in planning and building the Taj Mahal.

35
00:03:34.201 --> 00:03:38.374
The finest artisans and craftsmen were summoned from all over Asia.

36
00:03:38.375 --> 00:03:43.203
The chief architect was a Persian engineer and astrologer, Ustad Ahmad.

37
00:03:43.204 --> 00:03:53.182
Sculptors, designers of domes, stone carvers, calligraphers, accountants, and construction supervisors came from Persia, Syria, Turkey, and India.

38
00:03:53.183 --> 00:03:59.007
Similarly, building and ornamental materials were sourced from all corners of Asia and the Middle East.

39
00:03:59.008 --> 00:04:06.764
There was jade from China, turquoise from Tibet, and amber from Myanmarㅡover forty types of gemstones in all.

40
00:04:06.765 --> 00:04:11.018
The main building materials were white marble and red sandstone.

41
00:04:11.019 --> 00:04:20.007
Twenty thousand workers and one thousand elephants worked for twenty-three years and completed the Taj Mahal in 1653.

42
00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:27.409
Arjumand's tomb is situated almost three hundred meters from the entrance gate, at the opposite end of the surrounding garden.

43
00:04:27.410 --> 00:04:34.750
Four tall towers, or minarets, rise from its corners, and in the middle, there is a massive white dome.

44
00:04:34.751 --> 00:04:39.754
The tomb is made of white marble and has eight rooms inside on two floors.

45
00:04:39.755 --> 00:04:45.723
The central room is octagonal and contains the remains of Empress Arjumand and Shah Jahan.

46
00:04:45.724 --> 00:04:51.707
It was often said that the Taj Mahal was "built by Titans and finished by jewelers."

47
00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:58.464
The walls, floors, doors, and ceilings of the Taj Mahal and all its buildings are beautifully decorated.

48
00:04:58.465 --> 00:05:04.143
In one technique, gemstones are inlaid in the marble to make exquisite glittering patterns.

49
00:05:04.144 --> 00:05:10.287
In another technique, red pigment is used to create delicate floral patterns on the white walls.

50
00:05:10.288 --> 00:05:19.112
The Taj Mahal's entrance gate is richly decorated with floral patterns made from gemstones and with calligraphy in black marble.

51
00:05:19.113 --> 00:05:24.875
When you picture the Taj Mahal, you usually think only of the majestic, white-domed building.

52
00:05:24.876 --> 00:05:34.152
But in fact, everything around the tomb was designed to harmonize perfectly with it: the main gateway, the gardens, the mosque, and the rest house.

53
00:05:34.153 --> 00:05:41.678
A detached gateway is a feature of Muslim architecture. It is a symbol of the transition between Earth and paradise.

54
00:05:41.679 --> 00:05:44.749
A long garden runs from the gate to the tomb.

55
00:05:44.750 --> 00:05:50.092
The garden is Persian in style, with patterns based on the Islamic holy number of four.

56
00:05:50.093 --> 00:05:56.251
There are two marble canals that cross in the middle and divide the garden into four squares.

57
00:05:56.252 --> 00:06:00.752
The canals are lined with cypress trees, which symbolize death.

58
00:06:00.753 --> 00:06:06.753
Each of these smaller gardens is also sub divided by pathways into four flower beds.

59
00:06:06.754 --> 00:06:14.078
The garden was carefully designed so that there is a clear, unobstructed view of the tomb from any place on the grounds.

60
00:06:14.079 --> 00:06:22.228
At the center of the garden, halfway between the tomb and the gate, there is a raised marble tank containing water and lotus flowers.

61
00:06:22.229 --> 00:06:27.642
The tank was designed to perfectly reflect the Taj Mahal in its water.

62
00:06:27.643 --> 00:06:33.895
On either side of the tomb are red sandstone buildings; one is a mosque and the other is a rest house.

63
00:06:33.896 --> 00:06:38.730
Like all mosques, the mosque faces toward Mecca and is used for prayer.

64
00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:44.381
In front, a small piece of marble is polished so highly that it reflects like a mirror.

65
00:06:44.382 --> 00:06:51.375
The mosque floor sparkles with the color of red velvet and the ceiling is painted in a beautiful, hypnotic pattern.

66
00:06:51.376 --> 00:06:57.878
The other sandstone building is the twin of the mosque; it is identical in size and design.

67
00:06:57.879 --> 00:07:02.386
Because it faces away from Mecca, it has never been used for prayer.

68
00:07:02.387 --> 00:07:10.455
It was built as a rest house and for architectural balance, so that the tomb has two identical buildings on either side.

69
00:07:10.456 --> 00:07:18.845
The architects and craftsmen who worked on the Taj Mahal used some very special optical effects to enhance its grandeur and beauty.

70
00:07:18.846 --> 00:07:21.607
The first effect was the use of repetition.

71
00:07:21.608 --> 00:07:29.348
For example, the arch of the entrance gate is shaped like the tomb, so as you enter, you anticipate seeing its form.

72
00:07:29.349 --> 00:07:32.436
A second effect is due to the tomb's location.

73
00:07:32.437 --> 00:07:39.423
As you start walking toward it, it seems to grow in size, and the dome seems to slowly inflate.

74
00:07:39.424 --> 00:07:43.931
When you finally reach the base, it is colossal and magnificent.

75
00:07:43.932 --> 00:07:48.662
The other impressive effect is an optical illusion created by the calligraphers.

76
00:07:48.663 --> 00:07:53.909
Holy text from the Koran decorates many buildings, including the tomb itself.

77
00:07:53.910 --> 00:08:01.069
However, no matter how high the text is on a wall or ceiling, its size never seems to change!

78
00:08:01.070 --> 00:08:05.726
This effect was achieved by gradually increasing the height of the letters.

79
00:08:05.727 --> 00:08:10.401
Finally, the designers used nature to enhance the Taj Mahal's beauty.

80
00:08:10.402 --> 00:08:13.735
The sky is the background to the white marble building.

81
00:08:13.736 --> 00:08:20.551
Of course, this background constantly changes, and as the sky changes, the Taj Mahal changes too.

82
00:08:20.552 --> 00:08:25.391
At night, when the sky is dark, the Taj Mahal glistens in the moonlight.

83
00:08:25.392 --> 00:08:30.050
At sunset, when the sky is pink, the Taj Mahal softly glows.

84
00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:43.382
So, the Taj Mahal is captivating for many reasons: it is a monument to love, its architectural and artistic beauty is incomparable, and it has a spiritual quality that sets it apart from other buildings.

85
00:08:43.383 --> 00:08:49.206
When you see it you can't help wondering, does it belong to Earth or heaven?

